  "questions": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-75723",
      "knowledge_point_id": "43f839b4-2b0e-5ab5-809a-741747f1016d",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Power and Exponential Functions",
      "problem": "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1$ for $n>1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2",
              "option": "0",
