      "problem": "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges.",
