      "problem": "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}-1$ for $n\\ge 1?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡n}{{e}^{n/10}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$",
