    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-123715",
      "knowledge_point_id": "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function",
      "problem": "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n\\ge 1,$ converge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}}$\n\nII. ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}}$\n\nIII. ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}}$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}.$\"",
      "answer_fields": [
